 {"id":"CF-194","title":"P3 — the public-bind auth gate reads $HOME/.hermes/config.yaml while the gateway it launches resolves its data root from HERMES_HOME: it authorizes a file the server will not load and refuses a node that is correctly configured","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 by the code-factory-duckbrain-qa lane (tick code-factory-duckbrain-qa-2026-11-04-09-00-46) at origin/main e0e73bd, re-verifying the foreman-closed row CF-071.\n\nTHE MISMATCH. scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:69 hands check_public_bind_auth a hardcoded path, `\"$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml\"`, and :253 launches `nohup \"$VENV/bin/hermes\" serve --host 0.0.0.0 --port \"$PORT\" --skip-build` with no config argument and the caller's environment inherited. Hermes does not resolve its data root from `$HOME/.hermes` unconditionally: hermes_constants.py:194-201 defines get_hermes_home() as context override -> `HERMES_HOME` env -> platform default, and get_process_hermes_home() (:243-252) reads `HERMES_HOME` live; the served config is `<data root>/config.yaml` (hermes_cli/cli_config_load.py:265, `config_path = _hermes_home / 'config.yaml'`). So the gate can authorize one file while the server loads another.\n\nMEASURED, adapter fixture identical in shape to tests/selftest.sh::public-bind-auth (staged: /home/kara/.hermes/cache/scratch/cfqa-cfbrain-20261104-090046.mhMpIP/probe.sh):\n- Arm A (control, $HOME config with a real block): `PASS  gateway_start — /api/status -> 200`, launch reached, credentials leaked: 0.\n- Arm B ($HOME config with a real block, `HERMES_HOME` naming a directory whose config.yaml is `dashboard: {}`): `PASS  gateway_start — /api/status -> 200`, launch reached with `HERMES_HOME=<alt-unauth>`, and the product's own resolver under the same environment prints `resolved_hermes_home=<alt-unauth>`, `config_has_basic_auth_block=False`. The gate certified a file the launched gateway will not read — exactly the condition CF-071 exists to refuse.\n- Arm C (`HERMES_HOME` naming a directory whose config.yaml has the valid block, `$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml` comment-only): `FAIL  gateway_start — refused: … — commented`, launch NOT reached; the resolver names the alt directory with `config_has_basic_auth_block=True`. A correctly configured node is refused.\n\nNOT HYPOTHETICAL. The control arm's launch marker recorded `HERMES_HOME=/home/kara/.hermes` — the variable is exported in this fleet host's ambient environment (the adapter inherits it), and this host carries three profile data roots under ~/.hermes/profiles/ (eduos-e2e-tester, uncensored, voice), so a profile-scoped node's served config is a different file from the one the gate reads.\n\nWHY CF-071 DID NOT CATCH IT. Every arm of its fixture sets HOME and none sets HERMES_HOME (tests/selftest.sh:427-430), so the two paths can never diverge inside the suite; the row's acceptance criteria are about the content of one file, never about which file the product reads.\n\nCAVEAT, stated so the fix is not overclaimed: the resolver measured here is the host's Hermes build (v0.22.5), while the adapter pins 0.19.0. The row's criterion is therefore about the contract (the gate must read what the launched process reads) and about the adapter's own measured behaviour, not about a claim that 0.19.0 resolves identically.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce both arms in one throwaway fixture (stub `hermes --version` = 0.19.0, symlinked as `$HOME/hermes-venv/bin/hermes`): (a) `$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml` with a real basic_auth block and `HERMES_HOME` naming a directory whose config.yaml is `dashboard: {}` still prints `PASS  gateway_start`, and the launch marker records `HERMES_HOME=<that directory>`; (b) the reverse pair ($HOME comment-only, HERMES_HOME with the real block) prints `FAIL gateway_start — refused: … — commented` and does not reach launch.\n1. The gate reads the config file the launched process will read: with `HERMES_HOME` set, the evaluated path is `$HERMES_HOME/config.yaml`; with it unset, `$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml`. The resolved path appears verbatim in the gate's own row (both PASS and FAIL forms, e.g. `— config <path>`) so an operator can see which file was graded.\n2. Arm (a) is REFUSED and names the resolved path plus the reason (no basic_auth in that file); arm (b) reaches the launch path. Both are recorded in the evidence text with the resolver output that names the data root.\n3. A node whose `HERMES_HOME` points at a directory that does not exist, or whose config is unreadable there, is refused with a reason that names the path — never a silent fallback to `$HOME/.hermes`.\n4. tests/selftest.sh carries both arms as named rows in the public-bind-auth section plus a red mutation: a gate that hardcodes `$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml` again must make the suite fail, not pass.\n5. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` at the closing tip from a clean worktree of that tip.","probe":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T10:01:41Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T10:01:41Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"b33c64b110def123916ed79e9a7481d6157c27485244cacf0c79a16f04dc6a4e"}}
