 {"id":"CF-196","title":"P4 — two lanes minting the next event id from the same tip collide silently: events.jsonl carries duplicate id 336 and only a validator warning says so","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":3,"reasoning":"Measured by the sync lane tick code-factory-sync-2026-11-04-09-53-51 at published tip 4e18eb3, over 168 task rows and 337 events.\n\nTwo lanes appended to events.jsonl inside the same two-minute window and both computed their next id from the tip they had read:\n\n- id 336, event_type audit, task_id null, actor sync, timestamp 2026-11-04T10:08:06Z — this pass's own census event, landed at 4295860 and republished through bf3cdc0.\n- id 336, event_type task_created, task_id CF-195, actor foreman, timestamp 2026-11-04T10:09:59Z — landed at 24916c5 (board(qa): file CF-195) while this pass was pushing its verdict bundle.\n\n`bash scripts/boardctl validate` reports it as `[warn] events.jsonl line 337: duplicate event id 336 (also line 336) — benign legacy duplicates exist on live boards` and still prints `RESULT: OK (4 warning(s))`, so the gate that exists to check board integrity cannot fail on a duplicate id: the warning is indistinguishable from the pre-existing legacy duplicates and no counter counts them.\n\nThe writer is not at fault in either commit — each minted from the max it could read. The class is that nothing serialises the mint: `boardctl create`/`event` read the max id and append without a lock or a re-read, and the repo's own ship `scripts/board_lock.py` adjudicates a lock file that no writer takes. This pass deliberately did NOT renumber either line: both events are legitimate records, the ledger is append-only, and the second one belongs to a sibling lane mid-flight. It was left as a defect with an owner-to-be rather than a quiet edit.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n\n1. `jq -r .id .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | sort -n | uniq -d` prints nothing at the commit this row lands in, and the repair that removed the collision is recorded in a dated audit event naming which id moved, from what to what, and under whose authority — no line is deleted.\n2. `bash scripts/boardctl validate` stops printing the `duplicate event id` warning at that tree, so the warning class is empty rather than merely tolerated.\n3. A check in `tests/selftest.sh` fails on a fixture board whose events carry a duplicate id: with the duplicate present the arm prints a named FAIL row and the suite reports it, and with the fixture repaired the same arm prints PASS. The arm must be shown going red before it is trusted green.\n4. Either `boardctl create`/`boardctl event` re-reads the max id immediately before appending and refuses to write an id that appeared while it was composing (a `--force-id` opt-in may keep the current behaviour), or the repo documents in `docs/BOARD.md` that ids are advisory and a duplicate is expected under concurrency — with the measurement above cited. Silence is not one of the two outcomes.","acceptance_criteria":"1. `jq -r .id .coding-hermes/board/events.jsonl | sort -n | uniq -d` prints nothing at the commit this row lands in, and the repair is recorded in a dated audit event naming which id moved, from what to what, and under whose authority — no line is deleted.\n2. `bash scripts/boardctl validate` stops printing the `duplicate event id` warning at that tree, so the warning class is empty rather than merely tolerated.\n3. A `tests/selftest.sh` arm fails on a fixture board whose events carry a duplicate id (a named FAIL row, not a silent skip) and prints PASS on the same fixture with the duplicate removed; the red arm is shown before the green one is trusted.\n4. Either `boardctl create`/`boardctl event` re-reads the max id immediately before appending and refuses an id that appeared while it composed (a `--force-id` opt-in may preserve today's behaviour), or `docs/BOARD.md` states that ids are advisory under concurrency and cites the 336 collision measured above. Silence is not one of 