+{"id":"CF-197","title":"P4 — three docs still describe the installer's public-bind auth gate as a text-presence check: 3e6ac40 and 779ab4f made it a parsed non-empty credential check","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":1,"reasoning":"Measured 2026-11-04 by the code-factory-docs tick code-factory-docs-2026-11-04-10-01-07, first at published tip 4295860 and re-measured at bf3cdc0 after a sibling landed two rows mid-tick; every reading below is unchanged by that rebase. All arms ran in a throwaway clone; the registered checkout /home/kara/code-factory was left untouched because a live sibling hermes process (pid 2971297, started 04:54:53 local) held uncommitted work there (M docs/PRD-code-factory.md, M tests/selftest.sh, staged tasks.jsonl, M events.jsonl) - an ownership barrier, not a stale checkout.\n\nThe claim class: six sentences across three documents assert that `scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh` authorizes a public bind from TEXT PRESENCE of `basic_auth`. docs/OPERATIONS.md:80 (\"contains `basic_auth`\"), docs/OPERATIONS.md:82 (\"The current implementation uses a substring check for `basic_auth`\"), docs/THREAT-MODEL.md:46 (\"A substring `grep basic_auth` is not validation of effective configuration\"), docs/THREAT-MODEL.md:65 (\"refuses start unless `basic_auth` text occurs in config ... lines 61-69\"), docs/THREAT-MODEL.md:81 (\"the installer substring check is still insufficient\") and docs/THREAT-MODEL.md:93 (\"a config substring ... do not prove effective authentication\") describe that gate; specs/NODE.md:28 and :38 repeat the weaker form (\"carries no `dashboard.basic_auth`\"). At 3e6ac40^:127 the code was literally `if grep -q 'basic_auth' \"$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml\" 2>/dev/null; then`, so the prose was true until 2026-11-04.\n\nCommits 3e6ac40 (04:12, \"fix: parse Hermes public-bind auth - reject comments and empty credentials\") and 779ab4f (04:28, \"fix: validate Hermes auth with stdlib only\") replaced it: `check_public_bind_auth` (scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:66-186) runs a stdlib indentation-aware YAML reader and refuses with a named reason at :162 (config unreadable), :175 (invalid YAML), :180 (`commented` when only a commented `basic_auth:` line exists, else `block absent`) and :186 (`empty value (username and password must both be non-empty)`); the refusal reaches the `gateway_start` row at :258. The gate's only caller is :252, inside the branch that also starts the gateway, so it is skipped whenever a listener already holds the port (CF-192) and it reads `$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml` by path (CF-194).\n\nProof, run in a clone with fixture HOMEs and a stub `$HOME/hermes-venv/bin/hermes` (the adapter reports `already installed`, so no install happens): the published adapter refuses a comment-only config (`- commented`), an empty-credential config (`- empty value (username and password must both be non-empty)`) and a stray-text config (`extras:\\n  note: basic_auth` -> `- block absent`), and takes the start path on a valid block (no refusal row; curl reports `-> 000000` because the stub is not a server). The pre-fix adapter (3e6ac40^, staged beside the same scripts/lib.sh) starts `hermes serve` on ALL THREE of those configs - comment-only, empty-credential and stray-text - which is exactly the behaviour the docs still describe. A fifth arm reproduces the sibling's CF-193 limit independently: a valid `dashboard.basic_auth` block plus a `tools:` list refuses as `block absent (invalid YAML)`, so the corrected prose names that narrow-reader limit instead of implying the parser accepts arbitrary YAML.\n\nBoundary: CF-071 grades the parser and its own arms, and its closeout never swept the prose the parser invalidated (same class as CF-187, where a code fix left the runbook caveat standing). Distinct from CF-193 (the parser refuses ordinary YAML constructs) and CF-194 (the gate reads `$HOME/.hermes/config.yaml` while the gateway resolves its data root from HERMES_HOME) - those are code defects